## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** Thread.Abort patterns that all break on .NET 8:

| Pattern Found | Line | .NET 8 Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | `Stop()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `ProcessLoop()` | Never caught (never thrown) |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | `ProcessLoop()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (moved from ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop — replaces ResetAbort + loop condition
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Sleep + Abort
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

**1. `Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`**
The caller signals; the worker checks voluntarily. No forcible injection.

**2. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`**
The `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not unrelated ones.

**3. `Thread.ResetAbort()` → `break`**
ResetAbort prevented the exception from rethrowing so the thread survived. Now you just `break` out of the loop after cleanup.

**4. `Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(timeout)`**
Join actually waits for the thread to finish (or timeout). Sleep was a blind guess.

**5. `GetNextItem()` must accept a `CancellationToken`**
This is the **critical pitfall** — if `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue), cancellation won't interrupt it unless the token is passed through. For example:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel

// If using a custom wait:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout); // replaces Thread.Sleep or ManualResetEvent
```

## Common Pitfalls for This Pattern

| Pitfall | Why It Matters |
|---|---|
| Not passing token to `GetNextItem()` | Thread stays blocked indefinitely — cancellation never fires |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and callbacks; always implement `IDisposable` |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions |
| Assuming `Cancel()` stops the thread instantly | It only works at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
